<decompilertest>
<!--
    ptrdepthcap: a small-string-optimized object makes ActionInferTypes escalate
    a pointer type without bound (kuna RE-need c-string-objects-become).

    An SSO string object keeps EITHER the characters themselves OR a pointer to
    them in its first 8 bytes, chosen on the capacity field.  The compiler emits
    the two arms as a MULTIEQUAL over `p = &obj` (a PTRSUB off the spacebase,
    typed pointer-to-the-mapped-local) and `p = obj.ptr` (a LOAD from that same
    address, typed as the local itself).  That is the equation `T == ptr(T)`,
    which no finite type satisfies, so propagateOneType adds exactly ONE pointer
    level per pass and only stops at the empirical seven-pass settle ceiling.
    The object and every temporary that touches it are then declared with three,
    four or five levels of indirection.

    Stage model: S5 type propagation (`propagateTypeEdge`).  P0 assertion:
    `option ptrdepthcap on|off`.  The rule applied when on is upstream's own
    `TypeFactory::getTypePointerNoDepth` (type.cc:1509), which Ghidra already
    uses at LOAD/STORE but not at the spacebase-PTRSUB arm that drives this,
    hoisted to the single propagation funnel: a candidate whose target is itself
    a pointer-to-pointer collapses to `ptr(undefined<N>)`, a fixed point.  Depth
    1 and depth 2 over a concrete base are untouched.

    Layout (gcc -O0 -fno-stack-protector, five copies of the SSO pointer so the
    lattice has enough edges to run the escalation to its ceiling):
      0x100000  check: obj[3] = 0xf; fill(obj,in,5);
                       a=b=c=d=e=(char*)obj;
                       if (obj[3] >= 0x10) a=b=c=d=e=(char*)obj[0];
                       sink(a[0]); sink(b[1]); sink(c[2]); sink(d[3]); return e[4];
      0x100120  fill: ret        (stub)
      0x100130  sink: ret        (stub)

    Pass 1 (`option ptrdepthcap off`, upstream behaviour) reproduces the bug:
    the five copies are declared `char ****` (assert #1).
    Pass 2 (`option ptrdepthcap on`) settles the lattice: `char *`.  Assert #2
    pins EVERY three-or-more-star spelling across BOTH passes to exactly the 7 of
    the buggy pass (6 declarations + 1 cast), so any leak back into the capped
    pass raises the count and fails the test.
-->
